WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.990
In Season 3 of "The Mandalorian," Din Djarin
bathes in the Living Waters to restore his

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.950
status as Mandalorian.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:12.180
But his devotion to his clans means fans don't
get a view of everyone's favorite internet daddy.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.130
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:19.160
Although there are different factions of Mandalorian
people, the one that Din Djarin belongs to

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.359
is stringent when it comes to honoring the
old ways of the Mandalorians.

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:28.189
This means that Djarin is expressly forbidden
from removing his helmet in front of any living

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:33.030
being, for doing so would mean expulsion and
excommunication from his clan.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.800
However, Djarin does publicly remove his helmet
a few times throughout "The Mandalorian,"

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.700
either to say goodbye to his adopted child
or to save his own life.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.190
Because of these transgressions, Djarin is
forced to bathe in the Living Waters on the

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.379
ruined Mandalorian home planet to be readmitted
to his clan.

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:56.149
Mando achieves this goal, and it seems as
if he has taken his renewed vows very seriously.

00:00:56.149 --> 00:01:00.670
Djarin is never seen without his helmet throughout
Season 3 of "The Mandalorian," much to the

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.370
dismay of fans.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:06.650
Taking to social media, some fans lamented
that they weren't given a chance to see Djarin

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.960
without his helmet with one user tweeting,

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:16.010
"din took his helmet off twice in s2 because
he knew that tin can was staying ON for s3."

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:21.120
Many others agreed when it came to wanting
Djarin to take his helmet off at least once.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.350
On Twitter, a user posted an image of Kermit
the Frog browsing nooses and expressed tremendous

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.479
disappointment at not being able to see Djarin's
face.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:33.560
Likewise, one user wanted to see Djarin as
well and said that his helmet could have at

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.640
least fallen off during one of the many fights
throughout Season 3.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.479
These sentiments were also echoed over on
Reddit, where a Redditor started a conversation

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:46.520
by pointing out that this was the first time
Djarin hadn't taken off his helmet for an

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.300
entire season.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.490
This statement caused several to reply with
some saying,

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.630
"[Yeah] he shoulda had the helmet off at the
very end, just in the background.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:00.219
I thought they only took it off around family,
would have sold that even better."

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:04.430
Others agreed with that sentiment, thinking
that it would have been a nice touch to solidify

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.430
Grogu and Djarin as family.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:12.610
"Let it be written in song that Din Djarin
is accepting this Foundling as his son."

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:17.650
Another Redditor had their own theory as to
why Djarin never revealed his face this season.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.910
They thought he remained covered because Pedro
Pascal could only do the voice work since

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.290
he was also appearing in HBO's "The Last of
Us."

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:30.010
Even though many would love to see Djarin's
face, his spoken creed as a Mandalorian prevents

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.520
him from doing so.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.690
But most fans understand why it wasn't an
option for him in Season 3.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:40.870
As a Twitter user pointed out, the Mandalorian
takes his religion and culture seriously,

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.750
and he isn't going to risk being exiled again.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:48.270
This is especially true after having to go
through so much effort to redeem himself.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:53.989
Another user agreed, explaining that the bounty
hunter "went to Mandalore for a reason."

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:58.560
Elsewhere, some fans on social media believe
that Djarin keeping his helmet on in the season

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.050
finale actually made his enemies seem less
evil.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.230
The Twitter user wrote,

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.650
"i was waiting for them to take din's helmet
off to humiliate him when he was captured

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.900
but i guess moff gideon isn't as vile as i
expected him to be."

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:17.489
In short, for Djarin to show his face in public
again, he'll need to be captured by a truly

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:22.769
evil foe who not only wants to defeat him
but also wants to humiliate him.

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:27.939
If no villain is willing to sink that low,
then it would take an event so monumental

00:03:27.939 --> 00:03:32.390
that Din is willing to risk his status within
the Children of the Watch to show his face

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:32.890
once again.

